[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatches found and fixed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. Shift-left overflow in `cscan::make_f64` (real defect, fixed)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** Any hexadecimal input whose significand rounds *up* and carries
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m into a new binade aborted the Rust program in a `debug` build:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ printf '0x1.fffffffffffff8p0' | ./target/debug/driver
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m thread 'main' panicked at src/cscan.rs:462:27:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m attempt to shift left with overflow
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C output for the same input: `4000000000000000 0x1p+1 2.0000`, exit 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust `debug` output: empty stdout, a panic message on stderr, exit 101 — a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m mismatch on all three of stdout, stderr and exit status.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** `hex_to_f64` rounds the 53-bit quotient `q` to nearest/even. That
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m round-up can carry `q` all the way to `2^53`, i.e. 54 significant bits.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `make_f64` then computed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m let q_bits = 64 - q.leading_zeros() as i64;   // 54
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m let significand = q << (53 - q_bits);         // q << -1
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m a shift by `-1`. In a `release` build the shift amount is masked to 63, `q <<
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 63` overflows `u64` to `0`, the fraction field comes out `0` and the answer is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m *accidentally* correct (the carry case is always an exact power of two, so a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m zero fraction is what was wanted). In a `debug` build the overflow check fires
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and the process aborts. Either way the code was relying on wrap-around
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m behaviour that is not part of the contract.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** Re-normalise before encoding, so `q` never exceeds 53 bits:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m while 64 - q.leading_zeros() as i64 > 53 {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     q >>= 1;   // the carry can only produce a power of two, so no bits are lost
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     e2 += 1;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Inputs that reproduced it**, now covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `phase_c_hex_rounding_boundaries` and `phase_c_exponent_range_sweep_shard_4`:
